it is a challenging week it's the Easter  weekend now as you know it's a hot  weekend there's a lot going on in London  right across London and every day we've  had over a thousand officers in fact I  think it's probably now over 1,500  officers working to police these  protests it's had an impact not just on  the police obviously but on Londoners in  general lots of people have had  miserable disruption to their lives and  I've got one message for the protesters  and that is please go to Marble Arch  where you can protest lawfully stop  you're unlawful protest and if you don't  want to go to Marble Arch then go home  now what sympathy do you have for  businesses in the West End who by the  end of all of this will have lost tens  of millions of pounds in revenue I think  all all Londoners will have sympathy for  those businesses some will be able to  cope with their losses better than  others no doubt but it is clear that by  stopping for example Oxford Circus and  stopping the through route up from  Waterloo Bridge it's affecting business  in in all kinds of ways and people are  losing money it's also affecting people  who want to get about London whether  that's for work or tourist reasons it's  affecting everybody in the area of the  West End and I've been walking about  there today I can assure you that many  people are very fed up they're coming up  to me saying you know these protesters  don't realize they've you know the  impact they're having on people like me  have you been stung by the criticism  with people saying that this should have  been dealt with on day one police should  have just gone in and whatever a number  of arrests just kind of dealt with it  because of the impact on the city  commuters business and the international  reputation people looking in and seeing  this day after day have you been stung  by the criticism I'm very proud of what  the men have been doing I'm very proud  of the officers we're extremely  experienced in public order policing we  have fantastically experienced  commanders we have fantastically  experienced people on the ground we are  world leaders in public order policing  and in protest policing the guys and  girls have been working immensely hard  they have to work within the legal  framework and we have to have a  proportionate response  I'm sure people sitting in their  armchairs you know on one side or other  of the equation may feel I'd have done  it differently but I can assure you  they're thinking through all their  decisions the people on the front line  are more scrutinized probably than any  other profession in the world they're  having every single action filmed and  scrutinized and I think they're doing a  fantastic job and I'm really grateful to  them what happens in the days to come  we've heard your message to the  protesters to gold to Marble Arch and  protests where they were told to  peacefully and legally how much longer  will this last frame well that's up to  the protesters it to some extent isn't  it but we have already arrested over 700  people I think heading for 750 people we  will continue to arrest people we will  continue to dismantle street furniture  take away things that they put in our  way continue to try to minimize  disruption and get the roads moving and  allow people to go about their lawful  business and we will continue to do that  we have shown that we are strong we are  determined I've never I've been a police  officer for 36 years I have never known  an operation a single operation in which  over 700 people have been arrested it  shows we are determined and we will  carry on some reports that you've run  out of cells that you've run out of  officers to arrest people because  they're not cooperating the members of  the protests and it's taking four  officers to carry each person over and  they're arrested just can we talk about  resources and numbers have you run out  of cells  so what many of these protesters are  doing as you will have seen is either  gluing themselves or using very very  sophisticated mechanical contraptions to  make it extremely hard for the officers  to arrest them they therefore have to  spend quite a long time getting people  free using specialist equipment  specialist officers sometimes getting  the glue unglued and then the people are  refusing to walk so they are being  carried I think it is frankly amazing  and I'm very proud of the fact that  nobody has been injured as far as I can  see in any of these arrests but it does  take four or five officers  just to get somebody across to the van  and then they have to be transported to  ourselves  so that's resource intensive but we have  to do our job professionally and we are  doing it professionally once we're at  the custody Suites  of course there's a limited number of  cells in London we have a very good way  of managing the flow around the custody  Suites  we've still got cell space available at  the moment if it comes to the point  where we need to use cells in other  parts of the country and surrounding  forces then of course we would  and that's quite normal sometimes other  forces put people in our cells have you  spoken to the Prime Minister about  what's been going on I am NOT going to  tell you who and who I have spoken to  and who I haven't spoken to but I can  assure you I've spoken to many senior  politicians continue to do so I've got  an open line to both the mayor and  indeed to the Home Secretary they're  taking an interest what happens however  in terms of the operations on the ground  remain entirely the responsibility of  the Metropolitan Police British  Transport Police City of London police  and our partners and we will direct how  the protests are policed the buck stops  with me the protests haven't ended now  but will you be taking away learning  from what's happened over the past few  days in terms of this never happening  again London being effectively brought  to a standstill  so I think we should all pause we always  do after any large-scale or any  small-scale for that matter operation we  will pause in the met and have a good  think about whether there's anything we  could do differently what we've learned  from this how we adapt our tactics we  will also be talking to the government  and to our colleagues in the criminal  justice system to see whether there are  changes that should be made in the legal  framework to make it easier for us to  deal with people who are not violent but  are refusing to move when it is unlawful  for them to stay there protesting any  longer that is the dilemma  and if our powers are insufficient if we  don't have sufficient deterrence in the  criminal justice system then I will  certainly be asking for changes but I  don't think now is the time to go into  the detail of that we obviously need to  think about it  just looking ahead next weekend along  the marathon takes place a huge strain  on police resources as well so many  visitors coming in so many athletes what  happens if these protests are still  ongoing and you've got a world-class  event in the city taking up all of those  resources as well what's your message to  the protesters looking forward to the  next week well my message to protesters  thinking about Sunday marathon Sunday is  that I think a very very large number of  people right across the United Kingdom  would be furious if if the marathon was  affected in terms of its safety and  security or in any other way by protests  like these suffice to say the  Metropolitan Police is a huge service we  are continuing to run our business we're  here where you can see the emergency  calls are being answered we're dealing  with violent crime in all the boroughs  the officers are working extended hours  in order to be able to make that happen  we will continue to adapt and to flex  and continue if we need to with this  operation policing protests and if we  need to ask for help from others other  forces as we have done in the last  couple of days we will do so as I say  I'm very proud of what we've achieved  Heathrow has not been disrupted over 700  people have been arrested the officers  have been extraordinarily professional  and we will go on doing that and trying  to ensure that disruption to people in  general going about their business in  London is minimized as far as possible  the easiest way that can happen is if  these protesters go to Marble Arch we'll  go home so in all your policing  experience you've never known an  operation like this you you would never  do anything differently over the last  few days looking back I've said already  I think there will be bound to be things  that collectively we our partners people  in the criminal justice system and  indeed probably politicians in terms of  you know the legal framework will want  to think about how can we change we  always do that you know if I was running  a firearms operation today at the end of  it the guys and girls will debrief and  they'll say what did we learn from that  how will we do things differently I'm  proud of the professional and  proportionate response of my office  I'm proud by the determination the  resilience and the courage that they're  showing in the face of a quite a lot of  difficulty and pride at how dynamically  they're changing their approach when  they need to and the fact that we have  nobody who's been injured and 750 people  locked up
 

